---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'methods', label: 'Automated, hybrid, and manual' },
   { id: 'numbers', label: 'WCAG 2.2 criteria distribution' },
   { id: 'strategy', label: 'Testing hybrid criteria' },
   { id: 'lookup', label: 'Querying criteria & rules from the CLI' },
   { id: 'claims', label: 'Making credible accessibility claims' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Test methods"
   summary="<p>Accessibility compliance is not binary. In the <abbr title='Web Content Accessibility Guidelines: the W3C standard of testable criteria for digital accessibility'><a href='https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer'>Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG)</a></abbr>, every success criterion has an assigned <strong>test method</strong>: automated, hybrid, or manual.</p><p>Understanding this taxonomy is the difference between blindly assuming a clean automated scan means full compliance, and actually verifying real user experiences.</p>"
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="ledger" id="methods">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Methodology</p>
         <h2>Automated, hybrid, and manual test methods</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         WCAG is organized into numbered success criteria, such as <strong>4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value)</strong> or
         <strong>1.4.3 (Contrast Minimum)</strong>. <code>a11ied</code> classifies every criterion into one of three
         actionable test methods, derived from the official W3C Accessibility Conformance Testing (ACT) rules
         and Deque <abbr title="Open-source automated accessibility testing engine for HTML and DOM structure from Deque Systems"><a href="https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-core" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">axe-core</a></abbr> rule tags:
      </p>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>automated</code>
            <span>
               <strong>Direct rule evaluation</strong>. Software algorithms can definitively decide pass or fail
               by inspecting the DOM, styles, and computed values. For example, <strong>1.4.3 (Contrast)</strong>
               and missing image <code>alt</code> attributes in <strong>1.1.1 (Non-text Content)</strong> are fully automated.
               If <code>a1 audit</code> passes these checks, no manual verification is needed.
            </span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>hybrid</code>
            <span>
               <strong>Automated execution with human or agent verification</strong>. Static analysis can check
               syntactic prerequisites, but cannot verify whether interactive behavior is correct. For example,
               <strong>2.4.3 (Focus Order)</strong> and <strong>4.1.3 (Status Messages)</strong> require driving
               keyboard navigation or triggering dynamic updates to inspect spoken screen reader announcements.
            </span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>manual</code>
            <span>
               <strong>Human review required</strong>. Subjective or semantic requirements where no algorithmic
               heuristic exists. For example, <strong>1.3.3 (Sensory Characteristics)</strong> evaluates whether instructions
               rely solely on shape, size, or sound, and <strong>2.4.6 (Headings and Labels)</strong> evaluates whether
               descriptions are clear and helpful.
            </span>
         </div>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="numbers">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Coverage reality</p>
         <h2>WCAG 2.2 criteria distribution</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         Teams often assume automated scanners catch the vast majority of accessibility issues.
         Querying <code>a11ied</code>'s bundled WCAG data reveals the real numbers:
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 wcag criteria --summary</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>WCAG 2.2 test methods  updated 2026-09-05
  Level      Criteria   Automated  Hybrid     Manual     Unknown
  A          31         15         3          13         0
  AA         24         7          4          13         0
  AAA        31         5          2          24         0
  All        86         27         9          50         0

  28 criteria have an axe rule, 21 have an ACT rule, 19 have both.</code></pre>
      <p>
         At the standard <strong>Level AA</strong> compliance baseline (55 total criteria across Level A and AA):
      </p>
      <ul>
         <li>Only <strong>22 criteria (~40%)</strong> can be verified through automated static scans.</li>
         <li>The remaining <strong>33 criteria (~60%)</strong> are hybrid or manual, requiring interactive keyboard testing, screen reader observation, or editorial review.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
         This is why <code>a11ied</code> combines axe-core scanning with virtual and native screen reader automation:
         you can test the hybrid criteria that traditional CI linters miss entirely.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="strategy">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Actionable remediation</p>
            <h2>Testing hybrid criteria</h2>
         </div>
         <p>
            For hybrid criteria, <code>a11ied</code> provides concrete testing strategies that specify
            whether an interactive session is required:
         </p>
         <pre><code>a1 wcag 4.1.3 --json</code></pre>
         <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>\"testMethod\": &#123;
  \"method\": \"hybrid\",
  \"axeRuleIds\": [],
  \"actRuleIds\": [],
  \"sourceAttribution\": [\"strategy-heuristic\"]
&#125;,
\"strategy\": &#123;
  \"preferredEvidenceMode\": \"hybrid\",
  \"procedureIds\": [\"status_message_probe\"],
  \"requiresRealTarget\": true,
  \"notes\": [
    \"Status messages need announcement evidence from assistive technology without forced focus changes.\"
  ]
&#125;</code></pre>
         <p>
            When <code>requiresRealTarget: true</code> is returned, verifying HTML strings in isolation is insufficient.
            The test requires an active runtime session where you trigger an action (such as saving a form or updating a cart)
            and assert against the resulting speech queue:
         </p>
         <pre><code>a1 sr start --allow-virtual http://localhost:3000/settings
# Trigger update button...
a1 sr transcript --since 5s</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">AI agent feedback</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            When an AI coding agent fixes an issue, it inspects <code>strategy.procedureIds</code> to know
            which tool to invoke (e.g. running <code>sr_action</code> to test keyboard navigation instead of just re-running <code>axe</code>).
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="lookup">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Developer toolkit</p>
         <h2>Querying criteria & rules from the CLI</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         All WCAG specifications, Understanding documents, techniques, and rule mappings are bundled
         offline directly in the <code>a11ied</code> package. Queries run instantaneously without network requests:
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 wcag                                  # Interactive terminal finder
a1 wcag 2.4.3                            # Inspect requirement text, techniques, and test methods
a1 wcag G18                              # Query a specific WCAG technique
a1 wcag rule button-name                 # Map an axe violation to its WCAG criteria and fixes
a1 search "keyboard trap"                # Full-text search across all criteria and techniques
a1 pattern combobox                      # Query ARIA Authoring Practices Guide (APG) specifications</code></pre>
      <p>
         The CLI supports both <strong>WCAG 2.1</strong> and <strong>WCAG 2.2</strong> (default).
         Use <code>a1 search</code> when you know what behavior you need to verify but don't know the exact criterion number.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="claims">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Audit integrity</p>
         <h2>Making credible accessibility claims</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         A clean report from an automated scanner means that the elements on your page did not violate
         the subset of rules that automated algorithms can check. It does not prove that a user who relies
         on a screen reader or keyboard can successfully navigate your application.
      </p>
      <p>
         When reporting accessibility status to stakeholders or in compliance audits, distinguish between your evidence sources:
      </p>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>Automated axe scan:</strong>
            <span>Proves zero syntax or DOM contrast/naming violations across the ~27 automated criteria.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>Screen reader test suite:</strong>
            <span>Proves correct focus ordering, widget state announcements, and live region behavior across hybrid criteria.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>Manual evaluation:</strong>
            <span>Verifies cognitive, sensory, and content readability requirements that require human judgment.</span>
         </div>
      </div>
   </section>
</DocShell>
